  "query_preview": "..\nThursday and Friday: A closed upper level low over northern \nCalifornia wobbles northeastward into eastern Oregon Thursday,\nincreasing moisture over southeastern WA and the central ID \nPanhandle and bringing in cooler air aloft. Meanwhile, surface \ntemperatures will run 3-8 degrees warmer than on Wednesday. The \ncombination of more moisture, cooler temperatures aloft, and \nwarmer temperatures at the surface will result in steeper lapse \nrates and a more unstable atmosphere over the southeastern \nportion of the region during the afternoon and evening hours \nThursday. A 30-60 percent chance of showers will move in over\nsoutheastern WA and central ID Thursday evening with shower\nchances decreasing to around 20-40 percent late Thursday night\ninto Friday morning. There will also be a 20 percent chance for\nelevated thunderstorms primarily Thursday evening with the\nthreat of thunderstorms waning overnight as surface temperatures\ncool and CAPE values decrease.\n\nDowndraft CAPE of 800-1000 J/kg indicates outflow winds will be\nthe primary concern with convection Thursday. The HRRR model \ndepicts outflow winds up to 35-40 mph with convection, and \nthese gusts may extend far out away from the parent \nthunderstorm(s). One thing to watch for with these winds will be\nblowing dust, as planting season is well underway. Blowing dust\nmay lead to rapid changes to visibility across southeastern \nWashington. Secondary hazards will include brief downpours, \nsmall hail, and occasional cloud to ground lightning strikes.\n\nBy Friday, the upper level low tracks eastward into southern ID,\nmoving chances for showers and thunderstorms largely out of the\nregion.\n\nSaturday through Wednesday: Conditions look drier through the\nweekend and into early next week. The Inland Northwest will be \nunder a flat ridge with dry westerly flow bringing mostly sunny\nskies. Temperatures will be around five degrees warmer than \naverage for mid September with highs in the upper 70s to around \n80 for most valley locations through Monday. Temperatures cool \ninto the low to mid 70s by the middle of next week as a trough \napproaches the West Coast. /Fewkes",
